From 413c12e5d8b5c23f8a4598f585ea39a3cf36482d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:11:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] feat: invite a whole cohort from a spreadsheet
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Fifty accepted applicants sitting in Excel, and every row a real person
expecting to hear from the Lab. That shapes everything here.

READING THE SHEET

Paste the columns, or upload the .xlsx or .csv. Both arrive at one reader, so
there is one set of rules and one set of tests rather than a bug in whichever
path is used less.

Sheets are written by people for people, so the reader copes with what they
actually contain: a heading row, or none; the name column first or second; a
serial number, a phone and an organisation nobody asked for; blank spacer
rows; and the same applicant entered twice by two colleagues.

The address is found by shape rather than position, since it is the only cell
that is unmistakable. The name is then the nearest cell to it, looking left
first — sheets put a name beside the address it belongs to. Reaching for the
longest cell instead picks the phone number, which a test caught.

Nothing is ever dropped silently. A row that cannot be read is reported with
its number and its contents, because a dropped row is an applicant who never
hears from us and has no way of knowing why. Row numbers come from the file's
own row attributes, so one blank spacer row does not shift every reported
problem onto the wrong line of the admin's sheet.

An .xlsx is a zip of XML, which this codebase already opens for slide decks.
Reading the few tags needed is a page of code, and cheaper than taking on a
spreadsheet library for a feature that reads two columns.

SENDING

One request, but each person handled and reported on alone. A single typo, or
one applicant who signed up last week, must never stop the other forty-nine.
The response says what happened to every row, so an admin acts on the three
that failed instead of guessing which of fifty to retry.

Somebody who already has an account is enrolled directly rather than invited.
Somebody already on the programme is left alone. A live invitation is withdrawn
before a replacement is issued, or the first link stays valid forever with
nothing recording it. Re-running the same sheet after adding five names is
therefore safe, which is how this will actually be used.

Sending is sequential. Fifty at once is faster and is also the fastest way to
be rate-limited halfway through with nobody able to say who was invited.

Learners are enrolled on the programme when they accept, so their classes are
waiting rather than needing a second step from them or from the admin. An
invitation accepted after they enrolled themselves cannot overwrite the status
an admin set.

THE SCREEN

Nothing is sent until the admin has seen who was read out of their sheet, the
rows that could not be read, and how the total sits against the places on the
programme — an email cannot be recalled. Afterwards every person gets a line,
because "47 of 50 sent" leaves somebody hunting for the three.

Eleven route tests cover the parts worth guarding: a bad row not taking the
good ones with it, a provider refusal not stopping the batch, an existing
account being enrolled rather than invited, a repeated address getting one
email, and an accepted invitation never being reissued.

